1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:07,667
The whole journey: twelve chapters, each in one tiny picture.

2
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:12,467
Slopes first: how fast does the output move if an input moves?

3
00:00:13,067 --> 00:00:16,867
Several inputs: each one gets its own slope, its own sensitivity.

4
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:21,667
Then graphs: each number remembers its inputs, so we can walk back.

5
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:26,467
Order matters: a node goes backward only after all nodes after it.

6
00:00:27,067 --> 00:00:31,267
Gradients add up, and any operation works if its slope is right.

7
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:34,600
Tensors pack many scalars together, purely for speed.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,800
Then networks: layers of neurons, trained by gradient descent.

9
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:43,267
The loss surface is an analogy: real networks cannot be pictured.

10
00:00:43,867 --> 00:00:52,867
Every chapter had three acts. Keep that pattern for your own study.

11
00:00:53,467 --> 00:01:01,267
Our network had 41 parameters; real language models have billions.

12
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:06,000
They learn to guess the next word, with the very same arithmetic.

13
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,200
Remember: the gradient tells every knob which way to turn.

14
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:19,800
Here it is on one real knob: its gradient decides the step.

15
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:27,800
Next in Karpathy's series: makemore, a small language model.

